---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import { Grid, Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Introduction - Replenish Guide"
    next={{ name: "Card Analysis", href: "/analysis" }}
>
    <div>
        <h1
            style="margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.25rem; font-size: 3rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em;"
        >
            Replenish Guide
        </h1>

        <p
            style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); max-width: 650px; line-height: 1.6;"
        >
            The definitive guide focused on reading, visual clarity, and speed.
            Master every line of play with our optimized interface.
        </p>
        <figure
            style="margin: 0 auto; margin-bottom: 2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;"
        >
            <img
                src="/images/replenish.webp"
                alt="Replenish"
                style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; max-height: 600px; border-radius: var(--w-lg-radius); border: 1px solid var(--w-color-primary-50);"
            />
            <figcaption
                style="margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-30); text-align: center; font-style: italic;"
            >
                Replenish by Jim Nelson
            </figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div>

    <div
        style="margin: 2rem 0; padding: 1.5rem; background-color: var(--w-color-primary-80); border-left: 4px solid var(--w-color-primary-40); border-radius: 4px;"
    >
        <p
            style="margin: 0; font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--w-color-primary-20);"
        >
            <strong>Acknowledgments:</strong> This website is an expanded, interactive
            adaptation of the original Replenish Primer written by <strong
                >Brian Siu (Dr Siuss)</strong
            >. Huge thanks to Brian for allowing his work to be the foundation
            of this project. You can read his original primer at <a
                href="https://squeeshouseofpizza.com/en-brl/pages/replenish-primer-brian-siu"
                target="_blank"
                style="color: var(--w-color-primary-20); text-decoration: underline;"
                >Squee's House of Pizza</a
            >.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Introduction and History</h2>
    <p>
        Replenish is a combo-control archetype in Premodern Magic: The Gathering
        that leverages the namesake card Replenish to cheat enchantments into
        play from the graveyard. The deck’s lineage traces back to Standard of
        the early 2000s. In Premodern, Replenish has sustained a resurgence as a
        powerful and flexible strategy tunable toward aggressive combo kills,
        grinding midrange play, or long-game control.
    </p>
    <p>
        A critical rules change elevated the deck: the removal of old errata on
        the Parallax cards. After errata was reversed to match the printed
        cards, Parallax Wave and Parallax Tide now create delayed triggered
        abilities. If a Wave or Tide leaves play before the removal abilities
        resolve, the return trigger is severed—permanently exiling creatures or
        lands. Paired with Opalescence, this supercharged the deck’s combo
        lines.
    </p>

    <h2>The Parallax Tide Ban (January 2026)</h2>
    <p>
        On January 18, 2026, format curator Martin Berlin banned Parallax Tide.
        Thirty percent of top-8 decks across major 2025 tournaments were
        Tide-based archetypes. Replenish posted a 59.5% win rate in 2025—and
        when filtered against top-tier competition, it led the format at 54.9%.
    </p>
    <p>
        The community wrote Replenish off. The prevailing take: without Tide’s
        one-sided Armageddon, Replenish was a worse Enchantress.
    </p>
    <p>
        Those predictions have not held up. For me, over 248 post-ban matches,
        the deck has maintained a 72.3% win rate across the full metagame. On
        MTGO, Replenish has one of the most 5-0 league finishes. At the
        131-player Duress Crew New England Winter Regional - one of the first
        major paper events post-ban - Replenish placed 1st and 2nd, with three
        copies in the top 8.
    </p>

    <h2>Game Plan</h2>
    <p>
        The deck runs enchantments that are powerful but expensive. The primary
        plan is to discard them to cards like Attunement and Frantic Search,
        then cast Replenish to put them all onto the battlefield at once for
        four mana. But the deck can also just hard cast its enchantments
        naturally. Sometimes you win games without ever casting Replenish — you
        just deploy your pieces one at a time as you hit your land drops. That's
        more of a Plan B, but it comes up more often than people think, and it's
        part of what makes the deck resilient. Opponents who focus all their
        disruption on your graveyard can lose to a normally cast Opalescence
        into a normally cast Wave.
    </p>
    <p>
        The key combo is Opalescence plus Parallax Wave. Opalescence turns your
        enchantments into creatures. Parallax Wave can exile creatures by
        removing fading counters from itself. Since Wave is now a creature
        thanks to Opalescence, it can target itself. You stack a bunch of exile
        triggers targeting your opponent's creatures, then exile Wave with its
        last counter. Wave leaves play, comes back as a new object with fresh
        counters, and the exile triggers resolve — permanently removing those
        creatures because the Wave that exiled them no longer exists. Add Decree
        of Silence to that board and your opponent can't cast spells either.
        That's the lock.
    </p>
    <p>
        Everything else in the deck supports getting there. Swords to Plowshares
        buys time against early creatures. Intuition finds missing combo pieces.
        Meddling Mage disrupts opponents while you set up.
    </p>
    <p>
        The real strength of this deck is that it's not just a combo deck.
        Depending on the matchup, you might play it as a control deck, a
        midrange deck, or a creature-based beatdown deck after sideboarding.
        Meddling Mage adds another dimension to this. You can be proactive from
        turn two in ways that most opponents don't expect from an enchantment
        combo deck.
    </p>

    <h2>Strengths and Weaknesses</h2>
    <Grid column={2} gap="md" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <Card>
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-success);">
                Strengths
            </h3>
            <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Explosive wins:</strong> A single Replenish assembling
                    Opal-Wave-Decree ends the game.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Role flexibility:</strong> Combo, control, or midrange
                    depending on matchup. Meddling Mage adds proactive disruption.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Dominant against the field’s pillars:</strong> Enchantress,
                    midrange, creature strategies, and even most control decks are
                    favorable.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Post-ban positioning:</strong> Your opponents don't have
                    Tide against you anymore either, which frees up a lot of deckbuilding
                    space.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </Card>

        <Card>
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-danger);">
                Weaknesses
            </h3>
            <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Graveyard dependence:</strong> Vulnerable to Tormod’s
                    Crypt, Phyrexian Furnace, Planar Void. The creature sideboard
                    mitigates this.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Multi-axis aggression:</strong> If a deck is attacking
                    you from two angles at once, that's where things get hard. A clock
                    plus mana denial, or aggro plus counters. You need the right half
                    of your deck and there's no time to fix a bad draw.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Fast unfair combo:</strong> Decks like Devourer that goldfish
                    faster than you can interact are structurally difficult.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Mana hungry:</strong> Everything important costs three
                    or four mana. You have to survive to turn three before your card
                    selection comes online.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </Card>
    </Grid>

    <h2>Why Replenish?</h2>
    <p
        style="font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); line-height: 1.6;"
    >
        If you want to play a deck capable of explosive Turn-4 unfair combo
        kills, but that can simultaneously grind through long control matchups
        with heavy interaction and card advantage, Replenish is the deck for
        you.
    </p>
    <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            You enjoy combo decks requiring setup and patience with explosive
            payoff.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            You like intricate sequencing and decision trees.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            You want a deck that pivots roles based on matchup and board state.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            You want to punish Enchantress-heavy or midrange-heavy metagames.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            You appreciate the puzzle—figuring out the path to victory from each
            new position.
        </li>
    </ul>
</Layout>
